1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,490
Imagine a hurricane destroys
thousands of houses.

2
00:00:04,357 --> 00:00:08,887
People lose their homes. Roads are
damaged. Businesses are destroyed.

3
00:00:09,831 --> 00:00:12,001
And yet... GDP can go up.

4
00:00:12,807 --> 00:00:16,157
Wait. How can destroying things
make an economy look better?

5
00:00:18,017 --> 00:00:20,477
To understand that, we
need to talk about one

6
00:00:20,557 --> 00:00:22,967
of the most important
numbers in economics.

7
00:00:23,959 --> 00:00:25,159
GDP.

8
00:00:27,495 --> 00:00:30,365
GDP stands for gross domestic product.

9
00:00:31,281 --> 00:00:35,841
In simple terms, it measures the market
value of all final goods and services

10
00:00:35,921 --> 00:00:40,371
produced within a country's borders,
during a certain period of time.

11
00:00:41,063 --> 00:00:43,733
Usually, we talk about
a year or a quarter.

12
00:00:44,615 --> 00:00:47,855
So if factories produce cars,
companies provide software

13
00:00:47,935 --> 00:00:52,065
services, restaurants serve food,
builders construct houses...

14
00:00:52,903 --> 00:00:54,973
All of that contributes to GDP.

15
00:00:56,049 --> 00:00:59,519
GDP is basically an attempt
to answer one question.

16
00:01:00,429 --> 00:01:02,719
How much economic production is happening?

17
00:01:03,507 --> 00:01:05,797
And that's actually a
very useful question.

18
00:01:08,337 --> 00:01:10,787
So why does everybody care about GDP?

19
00:01:11,932 --> 00:01:13,712
Because it gives us a
reasonably standardised

20
00:01:13,792 --> 00:01:17,242
way to measure the size
and growth of an economy.

21
00:01:18,065 --> 00:01:22,115
If GDP is growing, the economy
is generally producing more.

22
00:01:22,919 --> 00:01:27,469
If GDP is shrinking, economic
activity is generally declining.

23
00:01:28,455 --> 00:01:31,585
And because we can calculate it
repeatedly, we can compare...

24
00:01:32,335 --> 00:01:35,075
this year with last year,
one country with another,

25
00:01:35,155 --> 00:01:37,665
or one period of economic
growth with another.

26
00:01:39,485 --> 00:01:41,675
So now we can go back to the hurricane.

27
00:01:42,631 --> 00:01:45,321
The hurricane destroys
houses. That's terrible.

28
00:01:46,226 --> 00:01:47,876
But then people have to rebuild them.

29
00:01:49,053 --> 00:01:50,593
Construction companies get paid.

30
00:01:50,673 --> 00:01:52,833
Workers get paid.

31
00:01:52,913 --> 00:01:54,303
Materials are purchased.

32
00:01:54,773 --> 00:01:56,803
Businesses provide services.

33
00:01:57,750 --> 00:02:01,700
The government may spend enormous amounts
of money repairing infrastructure.

34
00:02:02,774 --> 00:02:04,924
All of that is economic activity.

35
00:02:05,174 --> 00:02:08,744
And economic activity contributes to GDP.

36
00:02:09,805 --> 00:02:12,655
So GDP can increase after a disaster.

37
00:02:13,635 --> 00:02:15,355
But nobody would seriously argue that

38
00:02:15,435 --> 00:02:18,325
destroying a city made
its inhabitants richer.

39
00:02:19,171 --> 00:02:21,241
This is an important distinction.

40
00:02:21,971 --> 00:02:23,661
GDP measures activity.

41
00:02:24,730 --> 00:02:28,660
It doesn't automatically measure
whether that activity was good or bad.

42
00:02:31,600 --> 00:02:34,250
And that's not the only
weird thing about GDP.

43
00:02:35,323 --> 00:02:37,763
Imagine that a country
produces exactly the

44
00:02:37,843 --> 00:02:40,513
same amount of stuff
this year as last year.

45
00:02:41,179 --> 00:02:43,889
But everything becomes ten
percent more expensive.

46
00:02:44,901 --> 00:02:47,501
The country's GDP,
measured in current prices,

47
00:02:47,581 --> 00:02:50,851
can still increase by roughly ten percent.

48
00:02:51,419 --> 00:02:54,679
Did the economy actually produce more? No.

49
00:02:54,759 --> 00:02:56,229
Prices simply went up.

50
00:02:56,978 --> 00:03:01,828
That's why economists distinguish
between nominal GDP and real GDP.

51
00:03:02,813 --> 00:03:05,423
Nominal GDP uses current prices.

52
00:03:06,343 --> 00:03:10,153
Real GDP tries to remove the
effect of changing prices.

53
00:03:11,090 --> 00:03:15,170
So when you hear that an economy grew
by, say, three percent, you usually want

54
00:03:15,250 --> 00:03:19,720
to know whether that's real economic
growth, rather than simply higher prices.

55
00:03:22,740 --> 00:03:24,520
Another number you'll often see is...

56
00:03:24,600 --> 00:03:26,850
GDP per capita.

57
00:03:27,730 --> 00:03:30,920
Total GDP tells you how big an economy is.

58
00:03:31,687 --> 00:03:36,537
GDP per capita is more useful when
comparing people in different countries.

59
00:03:37,437 --> 00:03:41,657
And purchasing power parity, or
PPP, tries to account for the

60
00:03:41,737 --> 00:03:44,567
fact that a dollar doesn't buy
the same amount everywhere.

61
00:03:45,469 --> 00:03:50,639
But even GDP per capita adjusted
for PPP has a major limitation.

62
00:03:51,687 --> 00:03:55,677
It is an average. And averages
can hide enormous differences.

63
00:03:58,527 --> 00:04:01,397
GDP is also not the same thing as wealth.

64
00:04:02,247 --> 00:04:04,257
This is an easy mistake to make.

65
00:04:05,025 --> 00:04:08,835
GDP measures economic production
over a period of time.

66
00:04:09,537 --> 00:04:12,407
Wealth is about what has
accumulated over time.

67
00:04:13,153 --> 00:04:15,023
Think of GDP as a flow.

68
00:04:15,173 --> 00:04:16,983
And wealth as a stock.

69
00:04:17,817 --> 00:04:20,637
A country can produce a
huge amount every year

70
00:04:20,717 --> 00:04:23,327
without everybody
becoming equally wealthy.

71
00:04:24,035 --> 00:04:26,985
And a country can have
enormous accumulated wealth,

72
00:04:27,375 --> 00:04:30,745
even if its current economic
growth is relatively slow.

73
00:04:31,577 --> 00:04:34,427
So... GDP is not a country's bank account.

74
00:04:37,307 --> 00:04:39,757
And this is where things
start getting weird.

75
00:04:40,581 --> 00:04:42,651
Imagine you cook dinner for your family.

76
00:04:43,221 --> 00:04:45,231
You spend an hour preparing the food.

77
00:04:45,821 --> 00:04:47,351
Your family eats it.

78
00:04:48,020 --> 00:04:49,990
You've created something valuable.

79
00:04:50,975 --> 00:04:53,225
But as far as GDP is concerned...

80
00:04:53,835 --> 00:04:55,405
that hour didn't happen.

81
00:04:56,135 --> 00:05:00,785
Now imagine you pay a restaurant fifty
dollars to prepare exactly the same meal.

82
00:05:01,501 --> 00:05:03,321
Suddenly there is a market transaction.

83
00:05:03,401 --> 00:05:06,031
And GDP increases.

84
00:05:06,996 --> 00:05:09,626
The meal didn't magically
become more valuable.

85
00:05:10,505 --> 00:05:15,515
The economic activity simply became easier
for the statistical system to measure.

86
00:05:16,383 --> 00:05:19,793
The same thing happens with
many forms of unpaid work.

87
00:05:21,093 --> 00:05:22,253
Childcare. Cleaning. Cooking.

88
00:05:22,333 --> 00:05:24,303
Taking care of relatives.

89
00:05:25,302 --> 00:05:27,492
These things can be extremely valuable.

90
00:05:27,762 --> 00:05:30,352
But a lot of them don't appear in GDP.

91
00:05:32,582 --> 00:05:35,492
And this isn't a modern criticism of GDP.

92
00:05:36,582 --> 00:05:39,802
Simon Kuznets, one of the people
who developed national income

93
00:05:39,882 --> 00:05:44,052
accounting, warned about this
problem almost a century ago.

94
00:05:44,699 --> 00:05:48,079
In a nineteen thirty-four
report to the US Senate, he

95
00:05:48,159 --> 00:05:51,139
explicitly noted that the
welfare of a nation could not

96
00:05:51,219 --> 00:05:54,249
simply be inferred from a
measure of national income.

97
00:05:55,078 --> 00:05:58,928
And unpaid household work was among the
things left outside the measurement.

98
00:06:02,138 --> 00:06:05,128
And GDP doesn't tell us
whether people are happy.

99
00:06:05,903 --> 00:06:08,693
It doesn't directly tell us
whether people are healthy.

100
00:06:08,883 --> 00:06:10,773
Whether they feel safe.

101
00:06:10,983 --> 00:06:12,893
Whether they have free time.

102
00:06:13,619 --> 00:06:15,499
Whether the environment is clean.

103
00:06:15,579 --> 00:06:18,849
Or whether people actually
like the way they live.

104
00:06:19,669 --> 00:06:22,689
Two countries can have very
similar GDP per capita...

105
00:06:22,769 --> 00:06:25,799
and radically different quality of life.

106
00:06:26,622 --> 00:06:29,992
GDP simply wasn't designed
to answer those questions.

107
00:06:33,032 --> 00:06:35,722
And the hurricane wasn't
the strangest example.

108
00:06:36,563 --> 00:06:39,083
A country at war may produce
enormous quantities of

109
00:06:39,163 --> 00:06:42,493
weapons, while destroying
enormous amounts of wealth.

110
00:06:43,272 --> 00:06:45,142
All of the production is counted.

111
00:06:46,032 --> 00:06:48,022
None of the destruction is subtracted.

112
00:06:48,959 --> 00:06:51,049
There's a whole video about that one.

113
00:06:53,149 --> 00:06:58,539
Pollution, crime, accidents and disease
can all generate economic activity too.

114
00:06:59,219 --> 00:07:01,329
None of this means these things are good.

115
00:07:02,079 --> 00:07:05,069
It simply means that
GDP isn't a moral score.

116
00:07:05,909 --> 00:07:08,269
It doesn't give you points
for doing good things.

117
00:07:08,349 --> 00:07:11,659
And it doesn't subtract
points for doing bad things.

118
00:07:14,349 --> 00:07:17,519
So... is GDP a terrible
economic indicator?

119
00:07:18,519 --> 00:07:22,709
No. Quite the opposite.
GDP is incredibly useful.

120
00:07:23,503 --> 00:07:25,503
If we want to know whether
an economy is producing

121
00:07:25,583 --> 00:07:29,493
more or less, GDP gives
us valuable information.

122
00:07:30,511 --> 00:07:33,151
If we want to understand
recessions, recoveries

123
00:07:33,231 --> 00:07:36,101
and economic growth,
it's extremely useful.

124
00:07:36,900 --> 00:07:38,100
It is not perfect.

125
00:07:39,045 --> 00:07:41,225
But having a reasonably
consistent thermometer

126
00:07:41,305 --> 00:07:44,895
is much better than having
no thermometer at all.

127
00:07:45,584 --> 00:07:47,254
The problem isn't GDP.

128
00:07:48,411 --> 00:07:53,321
The problem is asking GDP to answer
questions it was never designed to answer.

129
00:07:55,981 --> 00:07:58,471
Think of GDP as a thermometer.

130
00:07:59,341 --> 00:08:02,191
A thermometer is great at
telling you the temperature.

131
00:08:03,170 --> 00:08:05,740
But you would not use it to
measure your blood pressure.

132
00:08:06,701 --> 00:08:08,301
And you certainly would not use it to

133
00:08:08,381 --> 00:08:10,671
determine whether you're
happy with your life.

134
00:08:11,469 --> 00:08:13,619
GDP works the same way.

135
00:08:14,381 --> 00:08:17,231
It tells us something
important about the economy.

136
00:08:18,082 --> 00:08:21,132
Just not everything that's
important about a society.

137
00:08:24,272 --> 00:08:25,772
So the next time you hear...

138
00:08:25,852 --> 00:08:28,482
"GDP increased by three percent."

139
00:08:29,497 --> 00:08:31,767
Don't immediately translate that into...

140
00:08:32,397 --> 00:08:34,407
"People are three percent better off."

141
00:08:35,127 --> 00:08:36,577
That's not what it means.

142
00:08:37,519 --> 00:08:40,289
It means that, according
to this particular measure,

143
00:08:40,499 --> 00:08:43,669
the economy produced more
economic value than before.

144
00:08:44,505 --> 00:08:46,215
And that's useful information.

145
00:08:46,985 --> 00:08:48,835
But it's only one piece of the puzzle.

146
00:08:49,723 --> 00:08:52,093
Because a country is
more than its economy.

147
00:08:53,019 --> 00:08:57,309
And economic growth is not automatically
the same thing as human progress.

148
00:08:58,257 --> 00:09:00,347
GDP is a thermometer.

149
00:09:01,211 --> 00:09:04,021
It was never meant to be a
complete medical examination.
